 little  bit loose but it's alright so to start  off I'm going to give you a 360 degree  view of the cubes and I'm going to  demonstrate what they can do and I'm  going to also give a short review of how  I think they can work basically I I've  been playing with them since yesterday  I've got to solve it a few times so I  you know what I think all right so this  is the V cube 6 it weighs 316 grams or  about 0.7 pounds it is actually one gram  heavier than the V cube 7 is most likely  because of the internal mechanism that  it has however it does seem to be a  pretty good weight it doesn't feel too  heavy or anything like that in the hand  and as far as the size it's about the  same size as a Rubik's Cube professor  this is 7 centimeters and this is 6.9 so  they're pretty close in size so if you  want a good comparison you could take a  look at a Rubik's Cube professor the  overall look of the cube is nice I like  the white plastic with the black  stickers it gives it a nice contrast the  color scheme of the rest of the cube is  identical to the Rubik's brand so  opposite of the red you have the orange  opposite of the green you have the blue  and opposite of the yellow in this case  we have the black instead of the white  as far as the structure of the cube it  seems relatively sturdy it does have a  screw holding everything together on  each side it is a little bit loose most  likely because it has a spring inside  that allows these layers to be somewhat  flexible as rotation is concerned as far  as rotation it does seem to have some  sort of snapping mechanism that allows  these layers to kind of click into place  seems to snap like 4 times per 90 degree  turn I don't know if you guys can hear  that but it's basically snaps at each  90-degree turn it clicks one of the  unique things I saw about the V cube 6  was its ability for these inside layers  to kind of turn independently of the top  layer so I've never seen that in any  other cube usually if you have a Rubik's  professor and you try to turn the inside  layer here the top layer will come along  with it you're not going to be able to  turn that layer by itself with one  finger so that I thought that was pretty  unique so the the overall turning is not  quite you know the smoothest that you'll  see however I feel that it should get  better  once you solve it a few times the  snapping mechanism is actually not bad  it allows you to kind of align the  layers a little bit better before  turning them however you got to be  careful because the the centerpiece is  here the center edge pieces will pop if  you're not too careful but the cube can  cut corners pretty nicely other than  that I think a little bit of breaking  the Kuban should should be good and you  should be able to get some pretty good  speed cubing times with it all right so  this is the V cube 7 as I said before it  weighs one gram less than the V cube 6  does which makes it a pretty decent  weight for its size it doesn't really  feel heavy at all because the cube is  obviously a little bit larger it's it  says 72 millimeters on the site I  believe that is from corner to corner  edge to edge it's about 77 millimeters  so the larger size does require that you  expand your hand a little bit and it  takes a little bit of getting used to  overall I like the look of the cube I  think it looks nice the pillow shape  makes it look nice as well makes it look  a little futuristic even though it's  basically for stability issues any cube  larger than 6x6 has to have that  pillowing shape otherwise it won't be  stable enough upon close inspection I  did notice that some of these outer  corner I'm sorry outer edge pieces they  did have some spacing in them it's not  that big of a deal and I tried to  squeeze them together but I think it's  pretty stable the way it is I don't  think it's really a problem the  structure overall is extremely sturdy I  mean I'm I'm pulling on that basically  as hard as I can and it doesn't really  move up here it does have a little bit  of give which is good because it allows  it to turn very well the the rotation is  extremely smooth and you can cut corners  like no problem at all  so I mean it is it is extremely stable  and it also sounds pretty nice as well  you might have seen everyone trying to  do the checkerboard pattern that's  basically just to show that each of  these layers turns pretty well with one  another I mean there's no like caching  in between any of these layers each of  them turns pretty well next to each  other so I think I think the cube  overall is really a masterpiece I think  it's a great cube probably I mean I know  some people are probably going to try to  duplicate the six and seven cubes but I  would say that this is going to be the  best one out it is a great cube I would  say for speed cubing there's really no  problems with this cube at all  occasionally I mean if you do try to cut  it if you try to cut it like that  obviously you're going to get some  pieces so that they're jammed but I  haven't had a situation where one of the  pieces has popped yet and I solved it  two times basically but it took me a  little while so I got to get used to  solving the bigger cubes as well overall  I think it's a really great cube so I  kind of like it a little bit better than  the 6x6 cube but I like them both  alright guys so for some final thoughts  on the B cube 6 and the B cube 7 I'm  going to have to say that of the - I  like the V cube 7 better it's just a  really nice design it's well made it is  stable smooth and durable there's really  not that much that I could say is wrong  with the V cube 7 it's pretty close to  perfection  as far as the V cube 6 is concerned I'm  not going to nitpick on small things  like the clicking of the layers it's  really not that big of a deal and you  get used to it and I'm sure that when  you solve it many more times it'll  probably not feel as clicky it'll  probably get worn in a little bit you  just have to break it in probably  but what kind of is annoying is the fact  that the pieces do pop out quite  frequently when your speed solving you  got to be careful in order to prevent  that from happening but it does happen  and you have to sit there the pieces you  know they fly out you got to pick them  up put them back in it's just a little  bit annoying other than that I think  that just the v-cube six is also a good  a good cube for speed solving I don't  think it should give that much problems  and like I said I think that it will get  better as you solve it more um both of  the cues are really well made overall I  would have to say that both of them are  worth the buy if you're going to buy it  to solve it by to collect it whatever if  you enjoy solving big cubes even with  the high price tag you might want to go  with both of them if you have to get one  then you're probably definitely going to  want to get the V Cube seven it's just  really a really well-made cube it's not  really any problems with the V Cube  seven at all alright guys so I probably  will have some speed solving videos  depending on how fast I am able to  eventually solve the cubes so you can  check out my channel at youtube.com  slash Rob h20 six to nine take care

